WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.259
In this video we are going to talk about
what is a MAC address. Let me remind you

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:08.580
this has got nothing to do with the Big
Mac or the Mac operating system! Now,

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.719
before we get into the details let me
give you an analogy: We have three people

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:17.789
John, Bob, and Mary in a room who want to talk. Now, John, when he wants to talk to

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:22.769
Mary, he says 'Hi, Mary! How are you doing?' Now, when Mary listens to this message,

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:26.519
how does she know that she has to
respond to John? It's through his voice.

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:33.030
So, she replies to John, but not Bob,
saying 'Hey, John! I'm doing good.' Now, if you

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.050
have three different computers who want
to talk to each other they don't have

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:43.829
any names, so to bring the analogy of
names and identification to computers we

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:49.649
have this concept of MAC address, which
is unique to each computer's network

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:50.850
interface card.

